  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Booz Allen Hamilton obligated $4.9B in FY2026 to date across 909 awards, with an average award size of $5.4M. Obligations are down 26.0% year over year. Yearly totals show a rise from $5.1B in FY21 to $7.1B in FY24, then a decline to $6.6B in FY25 and $4.9B in FY26. The General Services Administration is the largest buyer, obligating $2B, followed by the Department of Defense at $1.7B. Engineering Services and Computer Systems Design Services are the largest markets, at $1.4B and $1.3B respectively. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) is $677M, and All Other Professional, Scientific, and Technical Services is $553M. The largest single markets and the top buying agencies together explain most of the FY2026 to date obligations. This snapshot covers fiscal year to date totals and contract obligations; largest-award tables and expiring-contracts tables use award-value figures that represent total contract values, not obligations.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has Booz Allen's federal business trended recently?",
        "body": "Booz Allen Hamilton obligated $4.9B in FY2026 to date, down 26.0% year over year. Booz Allen Hamilton's obligations rose from $5.1B in FY21 to $7.1B in FY24, then fell to $6.6B in FY25 and $4.9B in FY26. The count and size of awards remain material, with 909 awards and an average award size of $5.4M. These figures reflect fiscal year to date obligations, not full-year totals."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Where is Booz Allen's FY2026 spending concentrated?",
        "body": "Booz Allen Hamilton's largest buying agency in FY2026 to date is the General Services Administration at $2B, followed by the Department of Defense at $1.7B. Booz Allen Hamilton's top markets are Engineering Services at $1.4B and Computer Systems Design Services at $1.3B, with Research and Development (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) at $677M and All Other Professional, Scientific, and Technical Services at $553M. Several civilian agencies also show meaningful obligations, including the Department of Health and Human Services at $296M and the Department of Veterans Affairs at $278M."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
